    "answer": {
      "short": "$A=1$, $B=-1$, $C=1$.",
      "worked": "Since $x^2+1>0$ for real $x$, multiply both sides by $x^2+1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-x+2&=A(x^2+1)+Bx+C\\\\x^2-x+2&=Ax^2+Bx+(A+C).\\end{align*}$$\n\nEquating coefficients gives $A=1$, $B=-1$ and $A+C=2$. Substituting $A=1$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}C&=2-1\\\\C&=1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, $A=1$, $B=-1$ and $C=1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
